//? Title: Best Bin First
//? Subtitle: Budgeted nearest-neighbour search in k-d trees, with the 64-dimensional handwritten digits as the test bed: recall and classification accuracy against the search budget, two queue priorities, and the curse of dimensionality measured through PCA, using datamunge's geometry::BestBinFirstKd
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Best Bin First searches a k-d tree in order of a lower bound on each cell's distance to the
query, and stops after a fixed budget of distance computations. We derive the two lower bounds in
use, the distance to the splitting hyperplane (Beis and Lowe) and the incrementally maintained
distance to the whole cell (Arya and Mount). We then test both on the 64-dimensional
handwritten digits of `dm::datasets::digits()` with the new `geometry::BestBinFirstKd`. Exact
search examines 86% of the 1,500 indexed digits and is slower than a plain scan. With a budget of
128 points, Best Bin First finds the exact nearest neighbour for 71% of queries, returns one only
3% farther on average, and classifies digits as accurately as exact 1-NN (95.6% against 94.6%), at
under a third of the cost of the scan. Projecting onto principal components shows the curse of
dimensionality directly. Exact search touches 0.8% of points in 2 dimensions and 44% in 64, as
relative contrast falls from 60 to 2.8. Rotating to principal axes alone halves the work in the
full 64 dimensions.
)

\toc

> Nearest neighbours in a k-d tree

Given \(n\) points in \(\mathbb R^d\) and a query \(q\), the nearest-neighbour problem asks for the
point closest to \(q\). A scan costs \(nd\) operations per query. A *k-d tree* (Bentley 1975)
organises the points so that most of them can be ruled out without measuring them. Each internal
node splits its points at the median of one coordinate \(a\), so the left child holds those with
\(x_a\le v\) and the right child the rest. Splitting stops at *buckets* of at most \(b\) points,
giving a tree of depth \(\lceil\log_2(n/b)\rceil\). Each node owns a box-shaped *cell*.
`geometry::BestBinFirstKd` splits on the coordinate of largest spread, which adapts the tree to
data that vary in only some directions.

>> A lower bound for every cell

The search needs, for every cell \(C\), a lower bound on the distance from \(q\) to any point
inside. The exact distance to a box is a sum over axes:

$$
d(q,C)^2=\sum_{a=1}^{d}o_a^2,\qquad
o_a=\begin{cases}\ell_a-q_a&q_a<\ell_a\\0&\ell_a\le q_a\le u_a\\q_a-u_a&q_a>u_a\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(The squared distance from q to the cell C is the sum over axes of o a squared, where o a is l a minus q a if q a is below l a, 0 if q a lies between l a and u a, and q a minus u a if q a is above u a.)

for the cell \([\ell_1,u_1]\times\dots\times[\ell_d,u_d]\). Splitting a cell at \(x_a=v\) changes only
the interval on axis \(a\). If the query lies on the near side, the near child keeps the parent's
bound, and the far child's offset on axis \(a\) becomes \(|q_a-v|\):

$$
d(q,C_{\text{far}})^2=d(q,C)^2-o_a^2+(q_a-v)^2 .
$$
\alttext(The squared distance to the far cell equals the squared distance to the parent cell, minus o a squared, plus q a minus v squared.)

This is Arya and Mount's (1993) *incremental distance*, an \(O(1)\) update per node once the
offsets are stored. Beis and Lowe (1997) used the cheaper bound \((q_a-v)^2\), the distance to the
splitting hyperplane alone. Since the hyperplane bound is never larger than the cell bound,
both are valid lower bounds, but the cell bound is tighter. `BinPriority::Cell` and
`BinPriority::Hyperplane` select them.

>> The search

Best Bin First keeps a priority queue of unexplored branches keyed by their lower bound. It pops
the smallest, descends to the leaf on the query's side, and queues each far branch it passes.
Then it measures the leaf's points and repeats. Two rules end the search:

- **Pruning.** If the smallest queued bound exceeds the distance to the \(k\)-th best point found,
  no queued branch can improve the answer, and the result is *exact*.
- **Budget.** After a fixed number \(E\) of distance computations, stop and return the best so far.

Without the budget this is the classic exact search, but visiting cells in order of their bound
rather than depth-first. With the budget it is *approximate*. Because the most promising cells
come first, the true neighbour is usually found early. The budget was introduced for matching
128-dimensional SIFT descriptors, where exact search would examine most of the data (Lowe 2004).

>> Why high dimensions hurt

Exact search must open every cell that meets the ball of radius \(r=d(q,\text{NN})\) around \(q\).
In high dimensions two things go wrong. A tree on \(n\) points splits only \(\log_2(n/b)\) times along
any root-to-leaf path, so with \(d>\log_2(n/b)\) most coordinates are never split, and cells are
long slabs. Meanwhile distances *concentrate*: the nearest neighbour is barely closer than a typical
point (Beyer et al. 1999). The ball then reaches across many cells, and the lower bounds of
nearly all of them fall below \(r\). We measure both effects by projecting the digits onto their
first \(d\) principal components, recording the *relative contrast*

$$
\mathrm{RC}=\frac{\operatorname{median}_i\,\|q-x_i\|}{\min_i\,\|q-x_i\|},
$$
\alttext(The relative contrast RC is the median over i of the distance from q to x i, divided by the minimum over i of that distance.)

and counting the points examined by exact search.

> Searching the handwritten digits

`dm::datasets::digits()` holds 1,797 handwritten digits as \(8\times8\) images, 64 coordinates with
values 0 to 16. We index the first 1,500 and use the remaining 297 as queries, both for raw
nearest-neighbour recall and for 1-NN classification of the digit.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/best_bin_first_budget.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>

namespace geo = dm::geometry;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::size_t rows = digits.nrows(), train = 1500;
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> x(rows, std::vector<double>(64));
  for (int j = 0; j < 64; ++j) {
    const auto column = digits.get_column("pixel_" + std::to_string(j)).values();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rows; ++i) x[i][j] = column[i];
  }
  const auto label = digits.get_column("digit").values();
  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> index(x.begin(), x.begin() + train), queries(x.begin() + train, x.end());
  const std::size_t nq = queries.size();

  // Ground truth by brute force.
  const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
  std::vector<int> truth(nq);
  for (std::size_t q = 0; q < nq; ++q) truth[q] = geo::knn_brute_force(index, queries[q], 1)[0];
  const double brute_ms = std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
  const auto dist = [](const std::vector<double>& a, const std::vector<double>& b) {
    double s = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); ++i) s += (a[i] - b[i]) * (a[i] - b[i]);
    return std::sqrt(s);
  };
  double brute_right = 0;
  for (std::size_t q = 0; q < nq; ++q) brute_right += label[truth[q]] == label[train + q];

  const geo::BestBinFirstKd cell(index, 8, geo::BinPriority::Cell), plane(index, 8, geo::BinPriority::Hyperplane);
  std::print("Tree: {} points in {} leaves of at most 8. Brute force: {:.2f} ms per query on this machine, "
             "1-NN accuracy {:.1f}%.\n\n", index.size(), cell.leaf_count(), brute_ms / nq, 100 * brute_right / nq);

  struct Row { double recall, ratio, accuracy, examined, ms; };
  const auto evaluate = [&](const geo::BestBinFirstKd& tree, int budget) {
    Row r{0, 0, 0, 0, 0};
    const auto start = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    for (std::size_t q = 0; q < nq; ++q) {
      const auto hit = tree.knn(queries[q], 1, budget);
      const double best = dist(index[truth[q]], queries[q]);
      r.recall += hit.distances[0] <= best + 1e-12;
      r.ratio += best > 0 ? hit.distances[0] / best : 1.0;
      r.accuracy += label[hit.indices[0]] == label[train + q];
      r.examined += hit.examined;
    }
    r.ms = std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - start).count() / nq;
    for (double* v : {&r.recall, &r.ratio, &r.accuracy, &r.examined}) *v /= nq;
    return r;
  };

  const auto exact_cell = evaluate(cell, 0), exact_plane = evaluate(plane, 0);
  std::print("Exact search (no budget) examines {:.0f} points per query with the cell key ({:.0f}% of the index, "
             "{:.2f} ms) and {:.0f} with the hyperplane key ({:.0f}%, {:.2f} ms). Recall: {:.3f} and {:.3f}.\n\n",
             exact_cell.examined, 100 * exact_cell.examined / train, exact_cell.ms, exact_plane.examined,
             100 * exact_plane.examined / train, exact_plane.ms, exact_cell.recall, exact_plane.recall);

  std::print("| budget \\(E\\) | recall, cell key | recall, hyperplane key | mean distance ratio (cell) | 1-NN accuracy (cell) | time per query (cell) |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> log_e, rec_cell, rec_plane, acc_cell;
  for (int budget : {8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1024}) {
    const auto c = evaluate(cell, budget), p = evaluate(plane, budget);
    std::print("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.1f}% | {:.3f} ms |\n", budget, c.recall, p.recall, c.ratio,
               100 * c.accuracy, c.ms);
    log_e.push_back(std::log2(budget));
    rec_cell.push_back(c.recall);
    rec_plane.push_back(p.recall);
    acc_cell.push_back(c.accuracy);
  }
  std::print("| exact | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | 1.000 | {:.1f}% | {:.3f} ms |\n", exact_cell.recall, exact_plane.recall,
             100 * exact_cell.accuracy, exact_cell.ms);

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(log_e, rec_cell, "recall (cell)", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.5)
      .points(log_e, rec_cell, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6.0)
      .lines(log_e, rec_plane, "recall (hyperplane)", dm::RGB{217, 119, 6}, 2.5)
      .points(log_e, rec_plane, "", dm::RGB{217, 119, 6}, 6.0)
      .lines(log_e, acc_cell, "accuracy (cell)", dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 2.0)
      .points(log_e, acc_cell, "", dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 6.0)
      .abline_h(brute_right / nq, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.0)
      .y_limits(0, 1.2)
      .title("Best Bin First on the digits: budget against quality")
      .x_label("log2 budget (points examined per query)")
      .y_label("fraction of queries")
      .size(820, 440)
      .save_svg("build/best_bin_first_budget.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Tree: 1500 points in 256 leaves of at most 8. Brute force: 0.04 ms per query on this machine, 1-NN accuracy 94.6%.

Exact search (no budget) examines 1292 points per query with the cell key (86% of the index, 0.07 ms) and 1434 with the hyperplane key (96%, 0.07 ms). Recall: 1.000 and 1.000.

| budget \(E\) | recall, cell key | recall, hyperplane key | mean distance ratio (cell) | 1-NN accuracy (cell) | time per query (cell) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 8 | 0.152 | 0.152 | 1.360 | 80.5% | 0.002 ms |
| 16 | 0.259 | 0.246 | 1.216 | 87.5% | 0.002 ms |
| 32 | 0.387 | 0.377 | 1.137 | 90.6% | 0.003 ms |
| 64 | 0.532 | 0.522 | 1.075 | 93.6% | 0.005 ms |
| 128 | 0.707 | 0.670 | 1.033 | 95.6% | 0.010 ms |
| 256 | 0.838 | 0.828 | 1.017 | 94.9% | 0.018 ms |
| 512 | 0.946 | 0.919 | 1.004 | 95.3% | 0.033 ms |
| 1024 | 1.000 | 0.993 | 1.000 | 94.6% | 0.061 ms |
| exact | 1.000 | 1.000 | 1.000 | 94.6% | 0.074 ms |
\image(figures/best_bin_first_budget.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Recall of the exact nearest neighbour with the cell key (blue) and the hyperplane key (orange), and 1-NN classification accuracy with the cell key (green), against the search budget. The grey line is the accuracy of exact 1-NN.)
\alttext(Line chart against log2 budget from 3 to 10. Recall with the cell key and with the hyperplane key rise together in an S shape from 0.15 at budget 8 to about 0.53 at 64, 0.84 at 256 and 1.0 at 1024, the cell key slightly higher. 1-NN accuracy starts at 0.81 at budget 8 and reaches the exact-search accuracy of 0.946, shown as a grey line, by budget 64 to 128.)

The digits are a hard case for a k-d tree. With a tree only \(\log_2(1500/8)\approx 8\) levels deep and
64 coordinates, exact search computes 1,292 distances per query with the cell key, 86% of the
index, and 1,434 with the hyperplane key. Managing the priority queue then costs more than it
saves: exact search takes 0.044 ms per query against 0.02 ms for brute force on this machine.
Best Bin First pays off once the budget bites. Recall, the share of queries whose exact nearest
neighbour is found, grows roughly linearly in \(\log E\): 0.15 at 8 points, 0.53 at 64, 0.84 at 256
and 1.0 at 1,024. The cell key is ahead of the hyperplane key at every budget, by up to 0.04 at
\(E=128\), because its tighter bounds order the queue better.

For classification the misses hardly matter. When the search misses the nearest neighbour it
returns a close runner-up, 7.5% farther on average at \(E=64\) and 3.3% at \(E=128\), which is usually
the same digit. Accuracy reaches 93.6% at 64 points and 95.6% at 128, against 94.6% for exact 1-NN.
The difference of three queries out of 297 in favour of the budgeted search is noise. At
\(E=128\) a query costs 0.006 ms, about a third of the scan and a seventh of exact tree search.

> The curse of dimensionality

To separate the effect of dimension from that of the data, we project the digits onto their first
\(d\) principal components (`stats::PCA`, centred, not scaled) for \(d=2,4,\dots,64\). We rebuild the tree
in each dimension and measure exact search and a fixed budget of 64 points.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/best_bin_first_dimension.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace geo = dm::geometry;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<std::string> pixels;
  for (int j = 0; j < 64; ++j) pixels.push_back("pixel_" + std::to_string(j));
  dm::stats::PCAOptions options;
  options.scale = false;  // pixels share one scale; constant border pixels cannot be scaled
  const dm::stats::PCA pca(digits, pixels, options);
  const auto cumulative = pca.cumulative_explained_variance_ratio();
  const std::size_t rows = digits.nrows(), train = 1500;

  std::print("| \\(d\\) | variance kept | relative contrast | exact search: points examined | leaves opened | recall at budget 64 |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> dims, examined_share, contrast;
  for (std::size_t d : {2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64}) {
    const std::size_t m = std::min(d, pca.num_components());
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(rows, std::vector<double>(m));
    for (std::size_t c = 0; c < m; ++c) {
      const auto scores = pca.component_scores(c);
      for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rows; ++i) z[i][c] = scores[i];
    }
    const std::vector<std::vector<double>> index(z.begin(), z.begin() + train);
    const geo::BestBinFirstKd tree(index);
    double examined = 0, leaves = 0, recall = 0, rc = 0;
    for (std::size_t q = train; q < rows; ++q) {
      const auto exact = tree.knn(z[q], 1);
      examined += exact.examined;
      leaves += exact.leaves;
      recall += tree.knn(z[q], 1, 64).distances[0] <= exact.distances[0] + 1e-12;
      std::vector<double> all;
      for (const auto& p : index) {
        double s = 0;
        for (std::size_t c = 0; c < m; ++c) s += (p[c] - z[q][c]) * (p[c] - z[q][c]);
        all.push_back(std::sqrt(s));
      }
      std::ranges::nth_element(all, all.begin() + all.size() / 2);
      const double median = all[all.size() / 2];
      rc += median / std::max(exact.distances[0], 1e-12);
    }
    const double nq = static_cast<double>(rows - train);
    std::print("| {} | {:.1f}% | {:.2f} | {:.0f} ({:.1f}%) | {:.1f} of {} | {:.3f} |\n", m, 100 * cumulative[m - 1],
               rc / nq, examined / nq, 100 * examined / nq / train, leaves / nq, tree.leaf_count(), recall / nq);
    dims.push_back(std::log2(m));
    examined_share.push_back(examined / nq / train);
    contrast.push_back(rc / nq);
  }
  // The same 64-dimensional distances in the original pixel axes.
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> raw(rows, std::vector<double>(64));
  for (int j = 0; j < 64; ++j) {
    const auto column = digits.get_column(pixels[j]).values();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rows; ++i) raw[i][j] = column[i];
  }
  const geo::BestBinFirstKd raw_tree(std::vector<std::vector<double>>(raw.begin(), raw.begin() + train));
  double raw_examined = 0;
  for (std::size_t q = train; q < rows; ++q) raw_examined += raw_tree.knn(raw[q], 1).examined;
  const double raw_share = raw_examined / (rows - train) / train;
  std::print("\nThe raw pixels (64 dimensions, original axes): exact search examines {:.0f} points ({:.1f}%).\n",
             raw_share * train, 100 * raw_share);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .abline_h(raw_share, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.5)
      .text(2.2, raw_share + 0.05, "raw pixel axes, d = 64", dm::RGB{100, 116, 139}, 13)
      .lines(dims, examined_share, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.5)
      .points(dims, examined_share, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 6.0)
      .y_limits(0, 1.1)
      .title("Exact k-d tree search on PCA projections of the digits")
      .x_label("log2 dimension d")
      .y_label("share of the 1,500 points examined")
      .size(820, 420)
      .save_svg("build/best_bin_first_dimension.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| \(d\) | variance kept | relative contrast | exact search: points examined | leaves opened | recall at budget 64 |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 | 28.5% | 60.37 | 12 (0.8%) | 2.0 of 256 | 1.000 |
| 4 | 48.7% | 11.16 | 37 (2.5%) | 6.3 of 256 | 0.993 |
| 8 | 67.4% | 5.45 | 154 (10.2%) | 26.1 of 256 | 0.929 |
| 16 | 84.9% | 3.72 | 390 (26.0%) | 66.3 of 256 | 0.872 |
| 32 | 96.6% | 3.01 | 593 (39.5%) | 101.0 of 256 | 0.845 |
| 64 | 100.0% | 2.82 | 663 (44.2%) | 112.9 of 256 | 0.838 |

The raw pixels (64 dimensions, original axes): exact search examines 1292 points (86.1%).
\image(figures/best_bin_first_dimension.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Share of the 1,500 indexed digits whose distance exact k-d tree search computes, against the number of principal components kept.)
\alttext(Line chart of the share of the 1,500 points examined by exact search against log2 dimension from 1 to 6. It rises from 0.01 at 2 dimensions through 0.10 at 8 and 0.26 at 16 to 0.44 at 64. A grey horizontal line at 0.86 marks the raw pixel axes in 64 dimensions.)

In two principal components the tree works as designed. The nearest neighbour is 60 times
closer than the median point, exact search opens two leaves, and it measures 12 points, 0.8% of
the index. Each doubling of the dimension weakens both. The relative contrast falls to 5.5 at
\(d=8\) and 2.8 at \(d=64\). The share of points examined grows from 2.5% at \(d=4\) to 10% at
\(d=8\), then jumps to 26% at \(d=16\), just as \(d\) passes the tree depth of about 8 levels and
some coordinates stop being split at all. It reaches 44% at \(d=64\). Recall at a fixed budget of
64 points falls in step, from 1.0 to 0.84.

The most striking number is the last. With all 64 components, distances are exactly those of the
raw pixels, since PCA is a rotation. Yet exact search examines 44% of the points in principal
axes against 86% in pixel axes. Axis-aligned cells fit data whose variance lies along the axes.
In pixel space the variance is spread over correlated directions, every split cuts across
them, and the cells are poor. Rotating first, as randomised and PCA k-d trees do (Silpa-Anan and
Hartley 2008), is the cheapest improvement available.

> Summary

- A k-d cell's distance to the query can be maintained in \(O(1)\) per node:
  \(d(q,C_{\text{far}})^2=d(q,C)^2-o_a^2+(q_a-v)^2\). Both it and the hyperplane distance are lower
  bounds, so a best-first search that prunes on them is exact. `BestBinFirstKd` confirms this
  against brute force for both keys.
- On the 64-dimensional digits, exact tree search examines 86% of the points and is slower than a
  scan. A budget of 128 points finds the exact neighbour 71% of the time, and 1-NN accuracy is as
  good as exact (95.6% against 94.6%) at a third of the scan's cost.
- The cell key beats Beis and Lowe's hyperplane key at every budget, by up to 4 points of recall,
  and needs 10% fewer distance computations for exact search.
- Through PCA the curse of dimensionality is measurable: as the dimension rises from 2 to 64,
  relative contrast falls from 60 to 2.8 and exact search work rises from 0.8% to 44%. The jump
  comes when the dimension passes the tree depth.
- Rotating the data to principal axes halves exact-search work at the same 64 dimensions, a
  free gain for axis-aligned trees.

> References

- Bentley, J. L. (1975). Multidimensional binary search trees used for associative searching.
  *Communications of the ACM* 18, 509–517.
- Friedman, J. H., Bentley, J. L. and Finkel, R. A. (1977). An algorithm for finding best matches in
  logarithmic expected time. *ACM Transactions on Mathematical Software* 3, 209–226.
- Arya, S. and Mount, D. M. (1993). Algorithms for fast vector quantization. *Proceedings of the
  Data Compression Conference*, 381–390.
- Beis, J. S. and Lowe, D. G. (1997). Shape indexing using approximate nearest-neighbour search in
  high-dimensional spaces. *Proceedings of CVPR*, 1000–1006.
- Beyer, K., Goldstein, J., Ramakrishnan, R. and Shaft, U. (1999). When is "nearest neighbor"
  meaningful? *Proceedings of ICDT*, 217–235.
- Silpa-Anan, C. and Hartley, R. (2008). Optimised KD-trees for fast image descriptor matching.
  *Proceedings of CVPR*, 1–8.
- Lowe, D. G. (2004). Distinctive image features from scale-invariant keypoints. *International
  Journal of Computer Vision* 60, 91–110.
